BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20261025T200000Z
DTEND:20261025T210000Z
LOCATION:Metz Carillon
SUMMARY:Meet Me at the Metz Carillon Series – Alex Johnson\, carillon
DESCRIPTION:About the ArtistAlex Johnson is currently the University Cari
 llonist of the University of Chicago (UC). There he teaches a studio of 2
 0 students and regularly plays the 72-bell\, 100-ton Laura Spelman Rockef
 eller Memorial Carillon at Rockefeller Memorial Chapel\, the third heavie
 st carillon in the world. Johnson performs carillon recitals and teaches 
 master classes across the world. He is an avid composer\, improviser\, an
 d supporter of new music\, too. At UC\, Johnson recently produced Tower T
 houghts\, the second Carillon New Music Festival and the world's first ca
 rillon festival featuring a talking tower\, featuring 25 composers\, 14 p
 erformers\, 11 world premieres\, and 46 pieces in total. Before moving to
  Chicago\, Johnson taught high school math in Austin\, Texas\, where he a
 lso taught and played the carillon at the University of Texas at Austin. 
 In 2020\, Johnson graduated summa cum laude from the Royal Carillon Schoo
 l "Jef Denyn" in Mechelen\, Belgium. In 2019\, he studied for six months 
 as the carillon fellow at Bok Tower Gardens in Lake Wales\, Florida. Also
  in 2019\, Johnson won several prizes at the 8th International Queen Fabi
 ola Carillon Competition in Mechelen: first prize overall\, prize for the
  best performance of a work by a Belgian composer\, and the improvisation
  prize. At the University of Rochester\, Johnson discovered the carillon\
 , played in the Mbira and Gamelan ensembles at Eastman School of Music\, 
 majored in physics\, and obtained minors in music\, math\, and linguistic
 s. During his undergraduate\, he also studied and played the carillon for
  one semester at the University of Sydney in Australia. Johnson's musical
  journey began with piano at age seven and percussion at age ten. He is d
 eeply indebted to his high school percussion instructors' time\, energy\,
  and talent. Besides music\, Johnson is fond of cooking and swimming in L
 ake Michigan.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>About the Artist</strong><br /><b
 r /><strong>Alex Johnson</strong> is currently the University Carillonist
  of the University of Chicago (UC). There he teaches a studio of 20 stude
 nts and regularly plays the 72-bell\, 100-ton Laura Spelman Rockefeller M
 emorial Carillon at Rockefeller Memorial Chapel\, the third heaviest cari
 llon in the world. Johnson performs carillon recitals and teaches master 
 classes across the world. He is an avid composer\, improviser\, and suppo
 rter of new music\, too. At UC\, Johnson recently produced Tower Thoughts
 \, the second Carillon New Music Festival and the world's first carillon 
 festival featuring a talking tower\, featuring 25 composers\, 14 performe
 rs\, 11 world premieres\, and 46 pieces in total. Before moving to Chicag
 o\, Johnson taught high school math in Austin\, Texas\, where he also tau
 ght and played the carillon at the University of Texas at Austin. In 2020
 \, Johnson graduated summa cum laude from the Royal Carillon School "Jef 
 Denyn" in Mechelen\, Belgium. In 2019\, he studied for six months as the 
 carillon fellow at Bok Tower Gardens in Lake Wales\, Florida. Also in 201
 9\, Johnson won several prizes at the 8<sup>th</sup> International Queen 
 Fabiola Carillon Competition in Mechelen: first prize overall\, prize for
  the best performance of a work by a Belgian composer\, and the improvisa
 tion prize. At the University of Rochester\, Johnson discovered the caril
 lon\, played in the Mbira and Gamelan ensembles at Eastman School of Musi
 c\, majored in physics\, and obtained minors in music\, math\, and lingui
 stics. During his undergraduate\, he also studied and played the carillon
  for one semester at the University of Sydney in Australia. Johnson's mus
 ical journey began with piano at age seven and percussion at age ten. He 
 is deeply indebted to his high school percussion instructors' time\, ener
 gy\, and talent. Besides music\, Johnson is fond of cooking and swimming 
 in Lake Michigan.</p>
